<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1672">1672</a>–<a href="/years/1725">1725</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Peter I, Father of the Fatherland · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">House of Romanov</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter the Great was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> and first Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, proclaimed emperor in 1721 after victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. He reorganised the army, built a navy, founded St Petersburg and subordinated the Church to the state, reorienting Russia towards Western European models. His victory ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>'s dominance in the Baltic, and his Russia treated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> as a client.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-the-great.jpg" alt="Peter the Great" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Jean-Marc Nattier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inconnu_d%27apr%C3%A8s_J.-M._Nattier,_Portrait_de_Pierre_Ier_(mus%C3%A9e_de_l%E2%80%99Ermitage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russia’s capture of Azov in 1696, though reversed in 1711 after <b>Peter the Great</b> was encircled on the Pruth, showed that Russian armies could reach the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the end of the grand duchy as a political form and the beginning of the Tsardom of Russia, which conquered Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan in 1556, became the Russian Empire under <b>Peter the Great</b> in 1721 and passed on Moscow's autocratic traditions to the modern Russian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles XII won at Narva in 1700 but was crushed by <b>Peter the Great</b> at the Battle of Poltava in 1709 and killed at Fredriksten in Norway in 1718.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Russia under <b>Peter the Great</b> and Catherine the Great treated the Commonwealth as a client state, intervening militarily to block reform and supporting compliant kings: Augustus II and Augustus III of the Saxon Wettin dynasty owed their thrones in large part to Russian arms, and Catherine secured the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Later princes had less room: Petru Rareș lost his throne and the Tighina (Bender) region when Suleiman the Magnificent invaded in 1538, and Dimitrie Cantemir’s alliance with <b>Peter the Great</b> ended in the Russian defeat on the Pruth in 1711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Russian Empire was formally proclaimed on 2 November 1721, when the Senate conferred upon <b>Peter I</b> the titles of Emperor of All Russia, Peter the Great and Father of the Fatherland, following his victory over Sweden in the Great Northern War (1700–1721) and the acquisition of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1706" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1706">What was here in 1706? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that the Swedes will have the advantage of us for some time, but they will teach us at length to conquer them.&#8221;</p><footer>After the defeat at Narva, 1700 (Voltaire's history) &#183; tr. Tobias Smollett, 1763 &#183; reported by Voltaire, History of the Russian Empire &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42540/pg42540-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To the health of my masters in the art of war.&#8221;</p><footer>Toast to the captured Swedish officers after Poltava, 1709 (Voltaire's history) &#183; tr. Tobias Smollett, 1763 &#183; reported by Voltaire, History of the Russian Empire &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42540/pg42540-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Countrymen and friends! what man is there among you, who could have thought, twenty years ago, that we should one day fight together on the Baltic Sea, in ships built by our own hands…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech after the naval victory of 1714 (Voltaire's history) &#183; tr. Tobias Smollett, 1763 &#183; the substance of the speech, as Voltaire gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42540/pg42540-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
